@spec mm_sort_order_spec  src=tests/specs/mm_sort_order_spec.lua  loc=171  sha=035a266  cases=7
@exercises midiManager (h.mm), midiBlob (midiBlob)
@surface   mm:modify, mm:notesRaw, mm:ccsRaw, mm:assign, mm:delete, midiBlob.parse
@harness   bareMM

# Intent
  Pins rebuild's ordering contract: after a modify, notes/ccs come back
  ppq-sorted, and a newly added event sits behind everything already at its
  ppq. Exercises both sort paths: the near-sorted insertion pass and the
  shift-budget fallback that a bulk reverse-order add trips.
  A ppq move lands among its new equals by the same rule as an add: one splice
  serves both, so the mechanism settles what design/stable-slots.md § Equal-ppq
  order left open for phase 1.

# Helpers
  @fn orderedPpqs(iter)  @ 12-16
  @fn sortedCopy(list)  @ 18-23
  @fn fieldAt(iter, ppq, field)  @ 27-31
      -- One field of everything sitting at one ppq, in walk order: exactly what the
      -- equal-ppq rule constrains, and nothing else.

# Cases
  @case 'a scattered add lands in ppq order (near-sorted fast path)'  [harness]  @ 35-47
  @case 'equal-ppq ccs keep insertion order across rebuilds'  [harness]  @ 51-64
  @case 'bulk reverse-order add trips the shift budget and still lands sorted, stably'  [harness]  @ 68-85
  @case 'a note added at an occupied ppq lands behind the note already there'  [harness]  @ 91-101
  @case 'a note moved onto an occupied ppq lands behind the note already there'  [harness]  @ 107-119
  @case 'a backwards ppq move lands behind its new equals too'  [harness]  @ 123-137
  @case 'equal-ppq wire order follows slot, not model order'  [harness]  @ 144-169

